LINEAR PROBING
linear probing means when we have collision
we use a linear function to find free index.
like just use:
newIndex = ( h(k) % m) + i ) % m

h()                   : the hash function
                        the function that convert our key to some value

m                     : array size (hash table size)

i                     : the umber of try
                        if we have a collision, at first try i =1
                        if he is not free, we should check next one (i=2)

h(k) % m              : base index
                        the index if we have not collision
                        we will use it

h(k) % m) + i         : just see the next indexes

( h(k) % m) + i ) % m : we need another "%m"
                        because by +i
                        we can reach the number above array size
                        so we should make it array cycle again!

Note:  (h(k) % m) + i ) % m
      =(h(k) + i) % m)
      so we use this simple representation!
      just think about it, it is correct!


SEARCHING
when we want to find the key
we exactly do the same!
we use "(h(k) + i) % m)" to find the indexes we should check
for this key!


PROBLEM
the linear probing has a problem called as "CLUSTER PROBLEM"
we put all the collisions side by side (because we put the value on next index),
so we will fill some indexes that are neighbors,
without any free index between them
we call these side by side indexes as cluster,
so if we have a new collision, we need traverse through this cluster!it is costly.
we need a function that distribute this newIndexes all over the array
to prevent create this clusters